Gr_monitor presents 3D color bar charts of all processes on a Linux
system grouped by user.  Charts are continuously updated by a piped
data feed.  There are controls for translating, rotating, scaling,
zooming, lighting and fog effects.

This is something I hacked together to learn something about OpenGL, SGI's
open standard for 2D/3D graphics. The whole thing was developed under
Linux using the Mesa, OpenGL look-a-like, library.  In a nutshell:

 - The output looks cute and might even be useful.  I was in two minds
   whether I was creating a useful utility or just trying to make pretty
   pictures out of what was going on on my Linux box.
 - It dominates the CPU on a 486 DX2/66 and is SLOW.
 - Statically linked to Mesa and GLUT libraries, so you don't need to
   get them to run it.
 - Preview JPEG at http://actrix.gen.nz/users/michael/michael.html.
 - The code is largly a cut up of other peoples work, so it's a bit messy.
 - Feedback welcome.

