**************************************************************************
This FAQ pertains to the OpenGL for Linux from Evans & Sutherland and is
based on the E-mail responses generated from the original announcement.
**************************************************************************

General Overview...

OpenGL for Linux is a straight port of the sample implementation OpenGL
code from Silicon Graphics. This port supports indirect software rendering
only. There is no support for direct hardware rendering. As you may guess,
it is not a speed demon. However, it is a robust environment for 3D OpenGL
application development. The intent of this project is to provide the Linux
community with OpenGL at the lowest cost possible. 

If you are currently running the XFree86 X11R6 server, you shouldn't have
any problems running OpenGL for Linux. All of the XFree86-3.1.1 binaries and
source are provided on the distribution. This package is in the older a.out
library format. OpenGL for Linux is encrypted, but this should not impact
use outside the U.S. and Canada. The encryption does not use DES. It is
written and maintained by E&S.  Please remember, this is your product. Your
needs and interest in this product will dictate its future.  As more
questions are posted, we will update this FAQ.


------------------------------------------------------------------------

Supported Server Overview...

The servers are based upon the XFree86-3.1.1 X11R6 release. The OpenGL
visuals are only available in the 8-bit mode. The OpenGL extension does not
use the Multi-Buffer extension. 

List of Supported Servers

	XF86_AGX_OGL
	XF86_Mach32_OGL
	XF86_Mach64_OGL
	XF86_Mach8_OGL
	XF86_P9000_OGL
	XF86_S3_OGL
	XF86_SVGA_OGL
	XF86_W32_OGL

List of Included Extensions

	BIG-REQUESTS
	GLX
	MIT-SCREEN-SAVER
	MIT-SHM
	MIT-SUNDRY-NONSTANDARD
	Multi-Buffering
	SGI-GLX
	SHAPE
	SYNC
 	X3D-PEX
 	XC-MISC
