2. On what media is OpenGL for Linux distributed?

OpenGL for Linux is distributed on CD-ROM. The file set is in the Rockridge
format. This format was used to preserve filenames and directory structures.

----------
3. When will OpenGL for Linux be available?

You may place your order now using any of the contact methods shown in
Question #1. The CD-ROM will be available to ship on June 19, 1995.

----------
4. What is OpenGL and why port it to Linux?

OpenGL is a vendor neutral application programming interface (API) for
developing 2D and 3D graphics applications that are highly portable to a
variety of computing environments from PCs to workstations to super
computers.  It provides a wide range of graphics features:  from rendering a
simple geometric point, line, or filled polygon to the most sophisticated
lighted and texture mapped NURBS curved surface.  

OpenGL consists of 250 routines to provide software developers with access
to these graphics capabilities:  geometric and raster primitives, RGBA or
color index mode, display list or immediate mode, viewing and modeling
transformations, lighting and shading, hidden surface removal (depth
buffer), alpha blending (translucency), anti-aliasing, texture mapping,
atmospheric effects (fog, smoke, haze), feedback and selection, stencil
planes and accumulation buffer.

OpenGL is the direct descendant of the IRIS Graphics Library.  IRIS GL was
invented in 1982 by Silicon Graphics (SGI) to provide a graphics development
environment for SGI systems.  In 1992 Silicon Graphics made the decision to
develop a version of IRIS GL that could run on all types of computers and
would become an industry standard for graphics application development.
Silicon Graphics began shipping OpenGL on its newest computers in late 1993.

It was ported to Linux to provide a low cost development alternative to
the high end market. This package provides everything to develop, compile
and run OpenGL programs.

----------
5. Is documentation included?

OpenGL For Linux ships with the complete man page set only. Because it is a
software rendering implementation, there are no hardware specifics to document. 

For someone new to graphics, help is readily and inexpensively available.
Addison-Wesley publishes The OpenGL Technical Library, a two volume set, to
make the learning process very easy. The OpenGL Programming Guide (ISBN:
0-201-63276-4) is your starting point. It provides step by step instructions
with plenty of code examples to walk you through the entire OpenGL, from the
first polygon to texture mapping and motion blur. The OpenGL Reference
Manual (ISBN: 0-201-63276-4) describes the technical operation of each
OpenGL routine. These books are available at your local bookstore, or
directly from Addison-Wesley (617-944-3700, x5030). For hands-on training,
OpenGL programming classes are available from the Silicon Graphics Technical
Education Department.

To keep the cost down, we do not include the Addison-Wesley books. To do so,
would nearly double the cost and many of you already own these books.
    
----------
6.  What is distributed in this release?

This release is a complete development set. You can develop, compile and run
OpenGL applications with this set. It includes the static libraries, include
files, sample and example code (demos), man pages and X servers. The X
servers are based on the XFree86-3.1.1 release. GLX is an extension to these
servers. The following servers are included with OpenGL for Linux:
    
		XF86_AGX_OGL
		XF86_Mach32_OGL
		XF86_Mach64_OGL
		XF86_Mach8_OGL
		XF86_P9000_OGL
		XF86_S3_OGL
		XF86_SVGA_OGL
		XF86_W32_OGL

These server names have an OGL extension after the standard XFree name.
There is also a LinkKit provided which will allow you to configure
and link your own OpenGL server. With future driver updates or as the need
for different extensions arise, you'll be able to create a new server with
the GLX extension. We have included one of the E&S demos called 'glaze'. The
glaze demo has its own man page for running options. Glaze shows off much of
the OpenGL functionality.

The XFree86-3.1.1 binaries and patched source code trees are also included
for your convenience. Except for examples, OpenGL source code is not included.

----------
7.  Can I develop OpenGL applications with this package?

Yes. OpenGL for Linux package includes everything you need to develop OpenGL
applications, although you may want to purchase Motif as well.

----------
8.  Is technical support provided?

OpenGL for Linux will not be supported in the classic sense. We will create
an alt.news group initially. This news group and others can be used as a
question and answer forum. We will accept bug reports, but immediate action
to solve them will not be taken unless they are fatal.

Because this is a software rendering implementation of the sample
implementation of OpenGL, the problems should be minor. If this were a
direct hardware rendering version, the risk of problems would be greater.
One of the biggest cost factors in any product is support. It is our desire
to keep the costs at a minimum and pass the savings to you.  

----------
9.  How do I install OpenGL for Linux?

The distribution is on CD-ROM. The CD file set format is Rockridge. Most
Linux distributions follow the ISO9660 format. You can still mount this CD
using DOS, but file names will be truncated. This CD will not work in the
umsdos file system. You will be able to mount the CD under your favorite
mount point (i.e. /cdrom) with the normal mount command (mount -r -t iso9660
/dev/xxxxx /cdrom ). Once mounted, just change directories to the mount
point and run the INST_OpenGL program. This program will ask for your
decryption key and then install the whole package. These instructions are
included with the CD-ROM.

----------
10. How much space will it take on my hard drive?

You will need about 14 megabytes of free space to install OpenGL for Linux.
The final installation size is about 10 megabytes.  

----------
11. Does it include the GLX X server extension?

Yes. See Question #6 for a list of the servers included with OpenGL for
Linux. The GLX extension is built into the XFree86 X11R6 servers.

----------
12. Does it include source code for OpenGL? 

No.  OpenGL is licensed from Silicon Graphics, Inc. 

----------
13. What hardware do I need to run OpenGL for Linux?

Most of the standard hardware supported by Linux will run OpenGL for Linux.
If you are currently running X on your Linux box, you shouldn't have any
problems running OpenGL. The OpenGL servers are drop in place. The
installation program will install and link X to your new server. No
configuration changes are required.

As a minimum configuration, you should run a 486 with 8 megabytes of RAM or
better. OpenGL has been tested on systems ranging from a 33 MHz 486 ISA with
an S-VGA server up to a 90 MHz Pentium PCI with a Mach64 server.

----------
14. Does OpenGL for Linux support direct hardware rendering?

No. This release is an indirect software rendering implementation. It will
not render directly into any hardware type. Tests show, however, that
performance is much better using accelerated video cards on a PCI bus system. 

----------
15. Does it support any 3D accelerated card like the ... ?

No. OpenGL for Linux does not currently support any hardware type in direct
rendering. If this release proves that there is a substantial interest in
OpenGL for Linux, we will consider a direct hardware port for the new 3D
cards coming to the market.  

----------
16. Does OpenGL for Linux support 15, 16 and 24-bit display?

No.  The color visuals OpenGL needs are not present in those modes.
The supported server depth is 8 bit.

----------
17. Is it faster than Mesa?

I have run Mesa and I think it's a good idea. I hope work continues with it.
As far as performance goes, some of Mesa is faster than OpenGL for Linux,
but it still needs work to be able to pass the ARB conformance tests. For
example, the last time I ran Mesa, it wouldn't do anti-aliasing or texturing. 

Right now the overhead with OpenGL for Linux is high. It's basically the
OpenGL sample implementation code as an extension to XFree86. It runs in
indirect mode, but because of the good accelerated drivers in XFree86, it
works really well, especially the S3 and Mach 64 servers.

----------
18. Do you have any performance numbers?

At the time of this FAQ we don't have any numbers. Most of the OPC numbers
would be meaningless for this version. There are so many different CPU and
video types that it would be impossible to test them all. The fastest machine 
tested was a 90 MHz Pentium with a Mach64 PCI video card. The Number
Nine S3 was also fast. Although there are no direct OpenGL hardware speed
ups, the accelerated video cards are definitely faster. 

----------
19. Can I generate OpenGL graphics on a SGI machine and display remotely
 via a PC X server running on one of the Linux PCs?

You should be able to display OpenGL clients from a remote host to your
Linux machine. This all depends on if the client conforms to the Linux
OpenGL server. This would be single buffered with an 8 bit depth.

----------
20. Will OpenGL applications developed on the Linux PC run on other types
of systems, such as Silicon Graphics workstations?

Applications developed on OpenGL for Linux should be fully portable to all
other OpenGL platforms. The glaze demo that ships with this release was
ported from other platforms to Linux in about 15 minutes. One of main
reasons why we did the OpenGL port for Linux was to provide a low cost
OpenGL platform for development. The other reason was to provide OpenGL to
those who want to learn OpenGL programming.

----------
21. Are site licenses available?

Site licenses for OpenGL for Linux are available to educational
institutions, such as accredited universities and high schools. To qualify
for the educational site license, please submit a request on your
institution's letterhead and fax it or mail it to Portable Graphics as shown
in Question #1.

Pricing is as follows:

2 Licenses:         $125
5 Licenses:         $275
10 Licenses:      $475
20 Licenses:      $790

Call for higher quantities.

----------
22. Is it a software implementation only, or does it support any accelerated
graphics cards?  How does this implementation render?

It is a software rendering implementation only. There are no hooks into any
hardware type. The faster the bus (i.e. PCI) and CPU, the faster your
applications will run. The accelerated servers such, as the S3 or Mach64
are faster than the S-VGA server. But this is because of the efficiencies of
the drivers for those servers.

The rendering works like this. The GLX makes calls to PolyFillRect or
PolyPoint. PolyFillRect is usually used to clear the screen between frames.
The rest is passed to PolyPoint. If you were to use a colored dithered
background your performance would suffer severely. A good example of this is
the 'olympic' demo in the sample code. It uses a dithered background and
takes about 2.5 minutes to run. If you change to a solid black background,
it runs in 20 seconds. This is documented in the README in the sample directory.

----------
23. Does OpenGL for Linux include an X/Motif interface similar to what is
provided by Silicon Graphics?

No. If you want Motif you will have to order it though one of the Motif
distributors.

----------
24. Does this version of OpenGL pass the OpenGL Architectural Review Board's
conformance compliance tests?

Yes. The servers that ship with OpenGL for Linux pass OpenGL compliance.
Note that one of the PCI cards tested did not pass. The Mach32 server failed
'must pass', but the VLB version of the card passed. It is not clear why it
didn't pass, unless there is some driver level bit changes happening.

----------
25. Will Open Inventor be ported to Linux?

Yes. The work for porting Open Inventor is underway at Portable Graphics, a
subsidiary of Evans & Sutherland located in Austin, Texas. Portable Graphics
is planning to demonstrate Open Inventor and OpenGL for Linux in booth 2151
at SIGGRAPH 95 in Los Angeles on August 8-10. 

----------
26. What is the LinkKit?

The LinkKit is the XFree86-3.1.1 Linkkit with the GLX libraries included. If
you install this kit you will be able to link your own OpenGL server. The
reason we include LinkKit is to allow you to keep pace with driver changes
and to enable different extensions other than the servers included with this
release. The servers shipped with OpenGL for Linux have most of the
available extensions linked in. If you want a smaller server you can cut it
down with the LinkKit. The extensions are ...

       	  BIG-REQUESTS
	  GLX
 	  MIT-SCREEN-SAVER
 	  MIT-SHM
  	  MIT-SUNDRY-NONSTANDARD
	  Multi-Buffering
 	  SGI-GLX
  	  SHAPE
  	  SYNC
  	  X3D-PEX
  	  XC-MISC
 	  XIE
  	  XTEST
	
----------
27. Are you planning to do faster implementations? If so why should I buy
    this one?

If the interest in this product is significant, Evans & Sutherland will
seriously consider doing a faster software port or a direct hardware port.
It is not practical to do a hardware port to the current 2D hardware on the
market. The time required to port to the multitude of graphics cards would
be tremendous. Any direct hardware ports would be targeted at the new 3D
cards coming to market, such as the Freedom Graphics PCI card.

If we offer a hardware-accelerated version of OpenGL for Linux in the
future, any customer who buys the initial software release will be highly
regarded. We will offer an upgrades to these customers for the difference in
cost between the hardware-accelerated version (price is not determined at
this time) and this version ($79) plus shipping and handling.
  

----------
OpenGL is a registered trademark, and Open Inventor is a trademark of
Silicon Graphics, Inc. 
E&S is a registered trademark of Evans & Sutherland Computer Corporation.

